;; confine.sb — the filesystem jail for the host SSH bridge (mktplace#15).
;;
;; Applied by container-build-gateway.sh as:
;;   sandbox-exec -D WORKSPACE=<declared workspace> -f ~/.local/state/multiplai/confine.sb …
;;
;; The workspace arrives as a PARAMETER, not baked in, so one shipped file
;; serves every install. It is read from ~/.local/state/multiplai/workspace,
;; which only the host can write — a value from the container would be a
;; boundary set by the side being confined.
;;
;; WHAT THIS IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
;;
;; It is a GUARDRAIL against accidental and stray writes: a build or a
;; transcription that names the wrong output directory, a tool that defaults to
;; $HOME, an argument the gateway's argv checks did not anticipate. Against
;; those it works, and that is the class of failure issue #15 actually
;; reported (`mlx_whisper --output-dir .` landing in ~).
;;
;; It is NOT a boundary against code the container controls. Two grants below
;; make that plain: `(allow mach-lookup)` is unfiltered, so confined code can
;; reach launchd and XPC services and have work done on its behalf by an
;; unconfined process, and `(allow process-exec)` is likewise unrestricted.
;; That is the standard seatbelt escape, and it matters here specifically
;; because `swift build` executes package-manifest and plugin code from the
;; checkout by design (README ▸ macOS host bridge says so). See the note at
;; the bottom of this file for what filtering would be required.
;;
;; SHAPE OF THE POLICY
;;
;;   writes   deny by default, allow back under the workspace and the caches a
;;            build genuinely needs. This is the control; everything else here
;;            exists to keep it from breaking real work.
;;   reads    open. The bridge's job is building and transcribing host-side:
;;            SDKs, toolchains, model weights and config all live outside any
;;            workspace, and enumerating them would be a second allowlist that
;;            drifts. Read confinement is not what issue #15 reported.
;;   network  open. mlx-whisper pulls models from Hugging Face on first use and
;;            the browser bridge speaks CDP over loopback.
;;   process  exec/fork allowed — a build spawns compilers, linkers, simulators.
;;
;; HOW IT GETS HERE
;;
;; multiplai-kit's setup.sh copies it with `install_host_state` (NOT
;; `install_host_tool`, which is for executables on $PATH) to
;; ~/.local/state/multiplai/confine.sb, mode 644, beside the workspace
;; declaration and the host-browser flag. It is data the gateway reads, not a
;; tool, and it lives in the one host-owned directory the container cannot
;; write.
;;
;; That copy comes from the kit's PINNED container/ checkout, so this repo must
;; tag first and the kit's CONTAINER_REF must then be bumped to that tag. A kit
;; pinned at an older tag finds no source file and installs nothing, silently —
;; the gateway keeps working with this layer simply absent.
;;
;; IF THIS FILE IS WRONG, EVERY BRIDGE COMMAND FAILS. sandbox-exec refuses to
;; run when it cannot compile the profile. Recovery is one command on the Mac:
;;   mv ~/.local/state/multiplai/confine.sb ~/.local/state/multiplai/confine.sb.off
;; The gateway treats a missing profile as "this layer is off" and keeps the
;; cwd pin and the argv path checks.

(version 1)

;; Deny by default. `(allow default)` would invert the whole point of the file.
;;
;; `(with report)` is NOT valid on a deny action — sandbox-exec rejects the
;; profile with "report modifier does not apply to deny action" and exits 65,
;; which fails every bridge command closed. Verified on a Mac 2026-08-17.
;; Denials reach the system log without it; watch them with
;;   log stream --predicate 'sender == "Sandbox"'
(deny default)
(deny file-write*)

;; --- what a confined command may do that is not a file write ---------------

(allow process-exec process-fork)
(allow signal (target same-sandbox))
(allow sysctl-read)
(allow mach-lookup)
(allow ipc-posix-shm)
(allow network*)
(allow file-read*)

;; The operations below are NOT optional extras — `(deny default)` denies every
;; operation nobody named, and each of these is something an allowlisted
;; toolchain reaches for. Without them the failure is an opaque sandbox denial
;; from deep inside a framework, not a message anyone can act on.
;;
;;   iokit-open / iokit-get-properties
;;     Metal. mlx-whisper is MLX on Apple Silicon and initialises a Metal
;;     device before it does anything else; the Simulator and Xcode's own GPU
;;     paths need it too. This is the grant whose absence bites first.
;;   process-info*
;;     xcodebuild inspects and reaps its children (pidinfo, listpids).
;;   system-socket
;;     route and DNS sockets — resolver setup, and anything that asks the
;;     kernel about the network.
;;   mach-register
;;     a process that checks in a service name of its own (test runners,
;;     simulator helpers) rather than only looking one up.
;;   file-ioctl
;;     terminal and device ioctls on files already opened under the rules above.
;;   pseudo-tty
;;     tools that allocate a pty for their own output handling.
;;
;; All additive, none of them widens the write policy — which is the control
;; this file exists for.
(allow iokit-open iokit-get-properties)
(allow process-info*)
(allow system-socket)
(allow mach-register)
(allow file-ioctl)
(allow pseudo-tty)

;; --- writes: the workspace ------------------------------------------------

(allow file-write*
  (subpath (param "WORKSPACE")))

;; --- writes: temporary directories ----------------------------------------
;;
;; Every toolchain here writes temporaries. /var/folders is where macOS puts
;; per-user TMPDIR, so omitting it breaks essentially everything.

(allow file-write*
  (subpath "/tmp")
  (subpath "/private/tmp")
  (subpath "/var/folders")
  (subpath "/private/var/folders")
  (subpath "/dev"))

;; --- writes: caches that are not workspace state ---------------------------
;;
;; Each entry below is a tool that writes outside any workspace by design, and
;; each is a real reported need rather than a precaution. They are listed one
;; per tool so that removing a tool from the allowlist makes the corresponding
;; write path obviously removable too.

;; Xcode / SwiftPM: DerivedData, module caches, SwiftPM's own clone cache.
;; `swift build` and `xcodebuild` both fail without these.
(allow file-write*
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/Library/Developer"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/Library/Caches/org.swift.swiftpm"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/Library/Caches/com.apple.dt.Xcode"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/.swiftpm"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/.cache/org.swift.swiftpm")))

;; mlx-whisper: downloads model weights to the Hugging Face cache on first use.
;; The issue calls this out explicitly as a thing that must keep working.
(allow file-write*
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/.cache/huggingface"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/.cache/mlx"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/.triton"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/Library/Caches/huggingface")))

;; qmd: maintains its search index outside the collections it indexes
;; (`qmd embed` / `qmd update` are on the gateway allowlist).
(allow file-write*
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/.qmd"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/Library/Application Support/qmd"))
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/.cache/qmd")))

;; A generic cache root, last and deliberately broad: several of the above are
;; conventions rather than guarantees, and a tool that moves its cache one
;; directory sideways should not take the bridge down mid-session.
(allow file-write*
  (subpath (string-append (param "HOME") "/.cache")))

;; --- what stays denied, on purpose -----------------------------------------
;;
;; Not listed above, and that is the point. Read this as "a stray write here is
;; stopped", not as "a determined process cannot get here" — see the escape
;; note below.
;;   $HOME itself                the reported escape — `--output-dir .` landing
;;                               in ~ because the forced command started there
;;   ~/.ssh ~/.aws ~/.config     credentials, readable but not writable
;;   ~/Library/Application Support/Google/Chrome
;;                               the browser profile. agent-browser is exempt
;;                               from this profile entirely (see the gateway's
;;                               agent-browser branch) precisely because
;;                               confining it here would be a contradiction —
;;                               but nothing ELSE gets to write it.
;;   /usr /etc /System /Library  the system
;;
;; The gateway also declares that the workspace may be neither `/` nor $HOME:
;; either value would make `(subpath (param "WORKSPACE"))` above hand back
;; write access to everything this list claims to deny.

;; --- KNOWN ESCAPE, unfixed, deliberately ------------------------------------
;;
;; `(allow mach-lookup)` at the top is unfiltered. A confined process can look
;; up any Mach service on the machine, including launchd's, and ask an
;; UNCONFINED process to act for it — the write policy above never sees the
;; write. `(allow process-exec)` is unrestricted in the same way. This is the
;; textbook seatbelt escape, not a subtlety of this profile.
;;
;; It is left open on purpose. Closing it means
;;
;;     (allow mach-lookup (global-name "com.apple.…") …)
;;
;; with a complete enumeration of what the Swift/Xcode toolchain, the
;; Simulator, MLX/Metal and the Hugging Face downloader each need — dozens of
;; names, several of them version-dependent — and an incomplete list does not
;; degrade, it breaks the tool outright with an opaque denial. That list cannot
;; be derived or tested anywhere except on a Mac with the real toolchains
;; installed, so shipping a guessed one would trade a documented gap for an
;; undocumented outage.
;;
;; To close it, someone with a Mac should: run each allowlisted tool under this
;; profile with `sandbox-exec` and the sandbox violation log open
;; (`log stream --predicate 'sender == "Sandbox"'`), collect the global-names
;; actually requested, and only then replace the bare grant. Until that exists,
;; the honest description of this file is the one at the top: a guardrail
;; against stray writes, not a boundary against code the container authored.
